WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.040
Part 4. You will hear a sports science student giving a presentation on the health benefits

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:12.920
of dance. First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:11.519
Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40.

00:01:11.519 --> 00:01:17.019
Dancing is something that humans do when they want to have a good time. It's a universal

00:01:17.019 --> 00:01:23.480
response to music found in all cultures. But what's only been discovered recently is that

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:31.839
dancing not only makes us feel good, it's also extremely good for our health. Dancing,

00:01:31.839 --> 00:01:39.080
like other forms of exercise, releases hormones, such as dopamine, which make us feel relaxed

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:46.419
and happy. And it also reduces feelings of stress or anxiety.

00:01:46.419 --> 00:01:53.120
Dancing is also a sociable activity, which is another reason it makes us feel good. One

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:59.739
study compared people's enjoyment of dancing at home in front of a video with dancing in

00:01:59.739 --> 00:02:06.699
a group in a studio. The people dancing in a group reported feeling happier, whereas

00:02:06.699 --> 00:02:15.580
those dancing alone did not. In another experiment, university researchers at York and Sheffield

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:21.300
took a group of students and sent each of them into a lab where music was played for

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:30.259
five minutes. Each had to choose from three options to sit and listen quietly to the music,

00:02:30.259 --> 00:02:38.699
to cycle on an exercise bike while they listened, or to get up and dance. All were given cognitive

00:02:38.699 --> 00:02:47.100
tasks to perform before and after. The results showed that those who chose to dance showed

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:52.339
much more creativity when doing problem-solving tasks.

00:02:52.339 --> 00:03:00.100
Dr Lovett, at the University of Hertfordshire, believes dance could be a very useful way

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:06.580
to help people suffering from mental health problems. He thinks dance should be prescribed

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:16.380
as therapy to help people overcome issues such as depression.

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:21.940
It's well established that dance is a good way of encouraging adolescent girls to take

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:29.380
exercise, but what about older people? Studies have shown that there are enormous benefits

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:35.539
for people in their 60s and beyond. One of the great things about dance is that there

00:03:35.539 --> 00:03:43.220
are no barriers to participation. Anyone can have a go, even those whose standard of fitness

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:51.500
is quite low. Dance can be especially beneficial for older adults who can't run or do more

00:03:51.500 --> 00:04:01.100
intense workouts, or for those who don't want to. One 2015 study found that even a gentle

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:08.619
dance workout helps to promote a healthy heart. And there's plenty of evidence which suggests

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:14.979
that dancing lowers the risk of falls, which could result in a broken hip, for example,

00:04:14.979 --> 00:04:23.220
by helping people to improve their balance. There are some less obvious benefits of dance

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:30.820
for older people too. One thing I hadn't realised before researching this topic was that dance

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:37.899
isn't just a physical challenge. It also requires a lot of concentration, because you need to

00:04:37.899 --> 00:04:45.179
remember different steps and routines. For older people, this kind of activity is especially

00:04:45.179 --> 00:04:52.100
important because it forces their brain to process things more quickly and to retain

00:04:52.100 --> 00:05:00.739
more information. Current research also shows that dance promotes a general sense of wellbeing

00:05:00.739 --> 00:05:07.940
in older participants, which can last up to a week after a class. Participants report

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:15.579
feeling less tired and having greater motivation to be more active and do daily activities

00:05:15.579 --> 00:05:24.019
such as gardening or walking to the shops or a park. Ballroom or country dancing, both

00:05:24.019 --> 00:05:30.820
popular with older people, have to be done in groups. They require collaboration and

00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:36.820
often involve touching a dance partner, all of which encourages interaction on the dance

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:45.339
floor. This helps to develop new relationships and can reduce older people's sense of isolation,

00:05:45.339 --> 00:05:53.660
which is a huge problem in many countries. I also looked at the benefits of Zumba. Fifteen

00:05:53.660 --> 00:06:02.459
million people in 180 countries now regularly take a Zumba class, an aerobic workout based

00:06:02.459 --> 00:06:10.339
on Latin American dance moves. John Porcari, a professor of exercise and sports science

00:06:10.339 --> 00:06:16.700
at the University of Wisconsin, analysed a group of women who were Zumba regulars and

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:26.459
found that a class lasting 40 minutes burns about 370 calories. This is similar to moderately

00:06:26.459 --> 00:06:34.980
intense exercises like step aerobics or kickboxing. A study in the American Journal of Health

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:42.779
Behaviour showed that when women with obesity did Zumba three times a week for 16 weeks,

00:06:42.779 --> 00:06:52.420
they lost an average of 1.2 kilos and lowered their percentage of body fat by 1%. More importantly,

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:59.940
the women enjoyed the class so much that they made it a habit and continued to attend classes

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:08.779
at least once a week, very unusual for an aerobic exercise programme. Dance is never

00:07:08.779 --> 00:07:15.100
going to compete with high-intensity workouts when it comes to physical fitness gains, but

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:31.619
its popularity is likely to keep on rising because it's such a fun way to keep fit.

00:08:08.779 --> 00:08:38.539
That is the end of the listening test. In the IELTS test, you would now have 10 minutes

00:08:38.539 --> 00:08:41.380
to transfer your answers to the answer sheet.

